    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes. Let me verify the full compile is clean (no unused-import or other warnings breaking the build) \u2014 it built successfully, so that's confirmed.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change is implemented and the test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures).\n\n**What I did** \u2014 modified `ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` so that every 400/409/429 rejection returns an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`:\n\n- Added a `problem(...)` helper that builds a `ProblemDetail` (via the existing `detailBuild`, which sets `type`, `title` from the exception name, `status`, and `detail`), attaches the offending field names as an `errors` property, and returns it with `Content-Type: application/problem+json`.\n- Converted all ten field-level handlers (`MissingOwnerFields`, `InvalidTelephone`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `FutureRegistrationDate` \u2192 400; `DuplicateTelephone`, `DuplicateEmail`, `DuplicateHousehold`, `DuplicateIdentity`, `CityAtCapacity` \u2192 409; `DailyRegistrationLimit` \u2192 429) from returning plain `Map<String,Object>` JSON to returning `ProblemDetail`. Each now takes the `HttpServletRequest` so the problem document's `type` URI reflects the request URL. HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n- Set `application/problem+json` explicitly on the `MethodArgumentNotValidException` (400) handler too, so bean-validation rejections (e.g. the missing-`city` case the new test exercises) are also RFC7807.\n- Removed the now-unused `java.util.Map` import; added the `MediaType` import.\n\nThe `errors` array (and, for validation failures, `schemaValidationErrors`) is preserved as a problem-document extension property, so existing field-level detail isn't lost.",
